[Event "Paris, at the opera"]
[Site "Paris FRA"]
[Date "1858.??.??"]
[White "Paul Morphy"]
[Black "Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]

{Played in a box at the Paris opera during a performance of Norma. Morphy's two opponents consulted together and still lost in seventeen moves. The whole game is a lesson in pins and in bringing every piece into the attack.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Bg4 {A pin on the knight. Morphy answers it at once.} 4. dxe5 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 dxe5 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. Qb3 {Two threats: Bxf7+ and Qxb7.} Qe7 8. Nc3 {Morphy could win a pawn with Qxb7 but prefers to develop. The queen on b7 would be out of play.} c6 9. Bg5 {The knight on f6 is pinned to the queen.} b5 10. Nxb5 {A piece for two pawns, to open the b-file and the a4-e8 diagonal.} cxb5 11. Bxb5+ Nbd7 {Now the knight on d7 is pinned to the king, and the knight on f6 to the queen. Black's pieces cannot move.} 12. O-O-O {Bringing the last piece into the attack, with the rook piling onto the pinned knight.} Rd8 13. Rxd7 Rxd7 14. Rd1 {The other rook takes over. The rook on d7 is pinned and attacked, and its defender, the knight on f6, is itself pinned.} Qe6 15. Bxd7+ Nxd7 16. Qb8+ {The queen sacrifice that made the game famous.} Nxb8 17. Rd8# 1-0
